WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.160
Two documented incidents
show that the whole-agency

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.412
view is a security property,
not a reporting preference.

3
00:00:06.412 --> 00:00:12.507
In May and June 2023, an actor used a
Microsoft consumer signing key to forge tokens

4
00:00:12.507 --> 00:00:18.512
and read Exchange Online mail across many
organizations, in some cases for weeks.

5
00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:21.737
The tokens were valid to every
system that checked them.

6
00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:24.417
What caught it was a
custom alert one federal

7
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:27.313
department had built on
mailbox logs it paid for.

8
00:00:27.313 --> 00:00:29.374
The second sits beneath the guest.

9
00:00:29.374 --> 00:00:33.961
ESXiArgs compromised thousands of
ESXi servers, and a later flaw handed

10
00:00:33.961 --> 00:00:38.700
full hypervisor control to anyone in
a directory group with the right name.

11
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.262
Even one vendor's two tools don't add up.

12
00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:45.396
ScubaGear is CISA's, the Zero
Trust Assessment is Microsoft's,

13
00:00:45.396 --> 00:00:48.313
and neither sees past
Microsoft's platform.

14
00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:50.024
Tools are evidence inputs.

15
00:00:50.024 --> 00:00:52.188
The agency owns the verdict.

16
00:00:52.188 --> 00:00:54.600
Next, the principles behind that.
